00:00:05.043 --> 00:00:06.960
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE: I
am Saptarshi Mukherjee.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.960
I head the product marketing
function for our data analytics

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:10.980
business.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:15.810
And today I have the privilege
to be joined by David Narayan,

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:19.350
and David is a distinguished
engineer from Home Depot, one

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:22.360
of our iconic
BigQuery customers,

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:25.710
and then Vivek Yadav, and
Vivek is a customer engineering

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.020
lead for Google Cloud.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.680
And today we're going to talk
to you about how enterprises

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:34.925
are reimagining what's
possible with a data warehouse

00:00:34.925 --> 00:00:36.300
and how they're
essentially going

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.270
beyond the conventional use
cases of operational reporting

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:42.210
and BI against
BigQuery and building

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:43.890
some of the things
that are incredible.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:45.720
Like, for example,
Home Depot built

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:50.400
a planet-scale application and
network log monitoring system

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.080
for their organization.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.590
So before we get into
the Home Depot story,

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.322
why are we trying
to reimagine what's

00:00:57.322 --> 00:00:58.530
possible with data warehouse?

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:00.810
And why data warehouse
is in the epicenter

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:02.165
of all this conversation?

00:01:02.165 --> 00:01:03.540
If you look at
fundamentally what

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.210
has happened over the course
of the last few years,

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:10.350
enterprises are experiencing an
astronomical growth with data.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.348
And Home Depot last
year talked about,

00:01:12.348 --> 00:01:14.640
how did they move from their
traditional data warehouse

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.720
system to BigQuery to handle
scale and have more agility?

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:20.640
And that is true for
many organizations,

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.473
because traditional
data warehouses were not

00:01:22.473 --> 00:01:26.280
designed for this amount
of data explosion.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.860
And the other thing is,
when enterprises are not

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:33.210
able to ingest, store, process
that large amount of data

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:35.970
not only coming from
traditional sources like ERP

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:40.960
or CRM system versus sources
like web clicks or IoT sensors

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:44.190
or application log, et cetera,
then they are essentially

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.490
creating a lot of data
silos and they're not

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.850
able to get the most
out of their data.

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:51.540
And this is a stat from
"Harvard Business Review."

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:55.650
It says, enterprises today
typically analyze less than 1%

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:59.720
of unstructured data and less
than 50% of structured data.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:03.160
And we are trying
to change that.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:04.420
Why data warehouse?

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:07.210
Because data management
and data analytics

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:11.500
is just not confined to
a warehousing platform.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:12.750
This is from Gartner.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:14.710
They are one of the leading
industrial analysts in the data

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:15.490
management space.

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:18.400
They say, fundamentally,
data warehouses

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:22.857
are still the foundation of a
company's analytic strategy.

00:02:22.857 --> 00:02:24.940
I started my career as a
data warehousing engineer

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:26.622
before I became a marketer.

00:02:26.622 --> 00:02:29.080
And I remember the days when
we used to kind of essentially

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:31.660
create very specific
operational reports

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:33.790
on a specific set of
dimensions and facts

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:35.250
for our business users.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.150
World has evolved a lot.

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:39.040
And we kind of
swung the other way

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:40.990
to start thinking
about data lakes

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:43.600
are going to be the
ultimate solution for a lot

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:45.233
of the big data problems.

00:02:45.233 --> 00:02:46.900
And now enterprises
are realizing, like,

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:49.300
hey, that possibly
is not the solution.

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:52.330
We need to refocus on
modernizing the data warehouse

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:56.020
to handle scale and address the
modern business requirements

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:57.700
from the warehouse.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:00.970
So as I was kind of
reflecting back, like,

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:04.300
what has been the evolution
of the data warehouse?

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:05.920
And data warehouse
is not a use case.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:07.420
And David is going
to talk about how

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.700
they are using the
warehouse to do something

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:11.950
that was not possible
before, like application log

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:12.850
monitoring.

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:14.650
In '90s, when
possibly many of you

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:16.900
started working on
warehouse, may not

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:19.870
have started working that
time, operational reporting was

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:20.830
the core essence.

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:23.890
And the way it was designed
was for a few users will

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:27.400
run a specific set of
queries that were predefined

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:31.850
and optimized, and this will
be the best performant queries

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:33.530
running against the warehouse.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:36.490
Then came the era of
2000 when enterprises

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:39.040
started realizing the
fact that customers

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:40.570
want to do ad hoc analysis.

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:43.210
They want to run
self-service BI.

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:45.447
Queries were not predefined.

00:03:45.447 --> 00:03:47.530
And business users were
able to essentially create

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:49.030
queries on the fly.

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:51.730
And the kernel that was
supporting the warehouse

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:54.970
needed to essentially
optimize queries on the fly.

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:58.780
In the early 2010, I would
say the era of data mining

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:01.960
came in, where it was not
just good enough to look

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:05.170
at the business from retrospect
what is happening on my sales,

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:06.820
but why certain
things are happening.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:08.860
So you will see a lot of
data warehouses started

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:11.620
adopting data mining
functions essentially.

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:14.380
Correlation, causality came in.

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:15.100
BigQuery.

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:16.540
We do support some of these.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.680
And I'm going to talk about it.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:22.510
And now as we stand
today, what is happening

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:24.820
with data warehouses
is businesses

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:27.640
are trying to get
predictive insights.

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:31.250
It's not just OK to look at
the data from retrospect.

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:33.160
It is important to
understand what's

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:36.760
going to happen to the
business based on the last X

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:38.800
number of years of
data or X days of data

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:40.660
that I have in my warehouse.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:43.120
So the evolution is really
important to understand.

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:46.690
So if you are operating
against a traditional warehouse

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:49.300
and you just want to
move it to Google Cloud

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:51.900
to do the same set of
use cases, possibly you

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:54.460
will underserve your business,
because a lot of things

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:57.400
that were not possible in
the warehouse in the past

00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:59.630
is possible today.

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:04.310
So I'm sure many of you have
already been using BigQuery.

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:05.470
Some of you are new.

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:09.490
BigQuery is our enterprise
data warehousing platform.

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:11.860
It's designed for
massive scalability.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:15.670
We are seeing customers who are
using BigQuery for terabytes

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:17.620
of data processing
to customers who

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:20.410
are using for hundreds of
petabytes of data processing

00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:22.180
using BigQuery.

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:24.203
It's highly encrypted, durable.

00:05:24.203 --> 00:05:26.620
And on the right side, if you
see, some of the things that

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:28.040
are unique to BigQuery.

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:29.335
For example, it's serverless.

00:05:29.335 --> 00:05:31.525
And in the Home
Depot example, we

00:05:31.525 --> 00:05:34.210
will hear why serverless
is so important,

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:37.990
because we have decoupled system
engineering from data analysis.

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:40.873
If you are streaming data
into the BigQuery service,

00:05:40.873 --> 00:05:42.790
you do not need to worry
about load balancing.

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:45.200
Like, hey, if I stream
data into the service,

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:47.290
is it going to impact
my query performance?

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:50.000
Is it going to impact
user experience?

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:54.310
Some of those automations have
been taken care of by Google.

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:57.168
Google BigQuery supports
real-time data analysis.

00:05:57.168 --> 00:05:59.710
And this is extremely important,
because data warehouses were

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:01.890
designed for batch paradigm.

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:05.020
And the reason why it was
designed was when you load data

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:09.070
into the warehouse, the kernel
cannot simultaneously address

00:06:09.070 --> 00:06:11.530
a lot of query requests
coming from multiple users.

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:13.540
That's why back
in the days when I

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:17.410
used to work as a data warehouse
person, load data on the night,

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:19.270
in the morning, 8:00
AM, fresh reports

00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:20.830
were served to
management people,

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:23.800
that paradigm has
completely shifted.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:27.070
Home Depot, for example, is
streaming data in real time

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:30.550
from multiple systems to
do analysis on that data.

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:32.070
King as an example here.

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:34.780
They're one of the largest
gaming companies, the makers

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:36.490
of the "Candy Crush Saga."

00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:40.420
This essentially has streamed
billions of events in real time

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:42.790
into the warehouse every
hour and do analysis

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:45.040
on gaming telemetry.

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:47.290
ML is built into BigQuery.

00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:49.600
BigQuery ML, you have heard
about the announcement.

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:51.730
We have gone beyond just
traditional regression.

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:54.400
We support now
[INAUDIBLE] clusterings,

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:56.920
matrix factorization, and
other algorithms for you

00:06:56.920 --> 00:07:01.280
to do lot of
ML-related use cases.

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:03.070
And then we announced BI Engine.

00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:07.000
And that's allowing customers
to do essentially concurrent

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:10.160
access on highly
accelerated data

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:15.460
sets for subsequent
queries on BI data sets.

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:17.140
So as I said, BigQuery
is serverless.

00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:18.515
I'm not going to
spend much time.

00:07:18.515 --> 00:07:20.560
I'm sure you have
seen versions of this.

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:23.410
Fundamentally,
BigQuery abstracts

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:27.100
a lot of the things on the gray
and the black that you see,

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:31.562
the monitoring, provisioning,
indexing, defragmentation.

00:07:31.562 --> 00:07:32.520
Nothing is [INAUDIBLE].

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:34.145
You essentially put
the data in and you

00:07:34.145 --> 00:07:36.680
start querying the data.

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:38.320
So this is at a
very high level what

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:41.030
BigQuery's architecture is.

00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:42.580
If you are familiar,
you know this,

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:45.940
BigQuery has a decouple
storage and compute layer.

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:47.940
So essentially, when
you stream data in

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:50.290
or you bulk load the data
into the storage system,

00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:53.320
the query engine can essentially
operate independently.

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:58.360
And it allows you to scale
those two things independently

00:07:58.360 --> 00:07:59.530
of each other.

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:00.970
It has a streaming buffer.

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:02.710
So that allows you
to essentially use

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:07.120
BigQuery's stream API to insert
up to 100,000 rows per second.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:09.000
We can essentially
increase those soft limits

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:11.020
if the requirement [? be. ?]

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:13.360
And then on the
right side, BigQuery

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:16.550
has a great programming
layer on top of it.

00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:18.390
So if you want to
programmatically access

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:21.160
BigQuery data from
the applications

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:24.543
for further analysis,
that's possible.

00:08:24.543 --> 00:08:26.710
So I wanted to kind of make
things real a little bit

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:29.020
and give you some
examples of how

00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:31.510
the traditional mold
of data warehousing

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:33.370
has been broken
by many customers.

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:35.210
And these are some
of the examples.

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:37.090
One example that comes
to my mind is Zulily.

00:08:37.090 --> 00:08:39.580
And they're an
e-commerce company.

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:43.720
They essentially stream in
up to 5 billion events a day

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:45.970
into BigQuery in real time.

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:49.570
And they use those
essentially user click events

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:52.870
on their website to do
personalized recommendations

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:55.570
for their product launches
that they do in their website.

00:08:55.570 --> 00:08:59.500
So again, an example, customers
are doing real-time analytics

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:01.820
against their data warehouse.

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:05.210
The second example
is 20th Century Fox.

00:09:05.210 --> 00:09:08.750
And 20th Century Fox
essentially is using BigQuery

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:10.520
to do predictive analytics.

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:14.060
They are doing user segmentation
for their new movie releases.

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:18.200
They're essentially bringing
in their marketing analysts

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:20.858
to do segmentation
using BigQuery ML

00:09:20.858 --> 00:09:22.400
and then disseminating
their insights

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:25.200
with their organization.

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:26.750
And then AirAsia.

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:28.910
They have been one
of our key customers

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:30.170
for the BI Engine launch.

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:32.660
They were doing alpha
testing with us.

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:35.180
And AirAsia is
essentially storing

00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:37.490
a lot of their important
data sets in BigQuery.

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:39.410
And now what they're
doing is they're

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:42.740
allowing their non-technical
business users, front line

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:48.500
users, to use Data Studio and
Sheets to connect with the data

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:49.832
and analyze that data.

00:09:49.832 --> 00:09:51.290
So essentially, if
you think about,

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:54.050
again, traditional
enterprise data warehouse,

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:57.710
few users playing with the data
from that multiple user groups

00:09:57.710 --> 00:09:59.880
are playing with the
same data simultaneously.

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:01.880
And there are many more
examples where customers

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:05.930
have done incredible job with
what's possible with the data

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:07.380
warehouse.

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:09.860
So it's time to
reimagine and see

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:13.010
something new that we haven't
talked in the past before.

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:16.970
And Home Depot team,
over the last year or so,

00:10:16.970 --> 00:10:20.540
built a planet-scale
application monitoring

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:22.200
system using BigQuery.

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:24.440
So they're using their
core data warehouse

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:27.300
to do something that we
haven't seen in the past.

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:30.110
So I'd like to welcome
David from Home Depot

00:10:30.110 --> 00:10:31.190
to share his experience.

00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:32.627
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:39.020
DAVID NARAYAN: Thank you, Sap.

00:10:39.020 --> 00:10:39.520
Hello.

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:41.750
It's good to be with
you this morning.

00:10:41.750 --> 00:10:43.960
So last year at Next, a
couple of my colleagues

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:46.840
were here talking about how we
migrated our enterprise data

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:48.800
warehouse to BigQuery.

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:51.520
And that was a huge effort,
but it was a tremendous success

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:53.483
for our company.

00:10:53.483 --> 00:10:55.900
So today I'd like to talk about
a completely different use

00:10:55.900 --> 00:10:56.650
case for BigQuery.

00:10:56.650 --> 00:10:58.283
It may be a little
bit nontraditional.

00:10:58.283 --> 00:10:59.950
But I would like to
talk about how we're

00:10:59.950 --> 00:11:02.730
using it for operational data.

00:11:02.730 --> 00:11:04.480
But first, just a
little bit of background

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:05.360
about the Home Depot.

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:08.030
We're the world's largest
home improvement retailer.

00:11:08.030 --> 00:11:11.200
We have about 2,300 stores
and about 150 supply

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:16.300
chain facilities across
the US, Canada, and Mexico.

00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:19.210
While we're a retail
company, technology

00:11:19.210 --> 00:11:22.900
is really at the center of
almost everything that we do.

00:11:22.900 --> 00:11:27.920
We're investing a lot of energy
and money into technology.

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:32.530
And in fact, 90% of the software
that runs our retail operations

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:34.690
is developed in-house.

00:11:34.690 --> 00:11:39.370
So with all of these sites
and systems and applications,

00:11:39.370 --> 00:11:42.217
they're generating a lot
of operational telemetry.

00:11:42.217 --> 00:11:44.050
So today I'm going to
talk about our efforts

00:11:44.050 --> 00:11:46.730
to build a pipeline to
collect and analyze all

00:11:46.730 --> 00:11:49.810
of that operational telemetry.

00:11:49.810 --> 00:11:52.540
So what do I mean by
operational telemetry?

00:11:52.540 --> 00:11:55.690
Often we think of that as
things like logs and metrics

00:11:55.690 --> 00:11:58.683
and traces from
applications, which is true,

00:11:58.683 --> 00:12:00.100
but we've expanded
that definition

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:03.550
to include things like
network flows, DNS queries,

00:12:03.550 --> 00:12:08.650
logs from system and servers
and network appliances,

00:12:08.650 --> 00:12:11.020
and also things like
associate systems,

00:12:11.020 --> 00:12:14.320
like registers and mobile
devices, in our stores

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:17.410
and supply chain facilities.

00:12:17.410 --> 00:12:18.890
So why collect all this data?

00:12:18.890 --> 00:12:20.330
What's the point?

00:12:20.330 --> 00:12:22.360
Well, a lot of this
data can be used

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:24.140
to help us understand
our environment,

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:26.145
or at least that's the
prevailing theory, right?

00:12:26.145 --> 00:12:30.180
But we've really thought about
it in terms of three use cases.

00:12:30.180 --> 00:12:32.338
So the first is the idea
of audit compliance.

00:12:32.338 --> 00:12:34.630
A lot of the data we collect
because we simply have to.

00:12:34.630 --> 00:12:36.940
We have to store the data
to meet our [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:36.940 --> 00:12:40.370
or PCI or other
sorts of regulations.

00:12:40.370 --> 00:12:42.190
The second reason
that we want this data

00:12:42.190 --> 00:12:44.878
is for monitoring
and observability

00:12:44.878 --> 00:12:46.420
or these traditional
use cases, where

00:12:46.420 --> 00:12:48.910
we want to make sure that
our teams have the ability

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:51.290
to understand what's going
on in their environment.

00:12:51.290 --> 00:12:53.290
And then the third, we
want to collect this data

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:57.550
and perform analysis on it
to derive longer-term trends,

00:12:57.550 --> 00:13:00.340
do capacity planning,
give us a sense of what's

00:13:00.340 --> 00:13:03.080
coming in the future.

00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:04.940
So who benefits from this data?

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:08.770
Well, one of the principles
that we established early on

00:13:08.770 --> 00:13:11.560
was that we wanted to
collect data once and allow

00:13:11.560 --> 00:13:13.750
multiple teams to
access that same data.

00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:16.510
It doesn't make sense to
recollect the same data over

00:13:16.510 --> 00:13:18.280
and over again.

00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:20.140
So we've identified
three teams that

00:13:20.140 --> 00:13:22.600
really find value in the
data that we're collecting.

00:13:22.600 --> 00:13:25.570
The first is our security
team, and it's not just

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:26.630
for audit compliance.

00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:29.110
We also collect data for
threat detection and incident

00:13:29.110 --> 00:13:32.650
response, so that they can
respond to things quickly.

00:13:32.650 --> 00:13:35.150
The second team is our
application developers.

00:13:35.150 --> 00:13:37.482
They use the data for
troubleshooting and diagnosing

00:13:37.482 --> 00:13:39.190
their systems, their
applications, what's

00:13:39.190 --> 00:13:40.810
going on in their environment.

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:42.610
And then the third would be our
infrastructure and operations

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:43.310
team.

00:13:43.310 --> 00:13:45.530
They also use the data to
monitor their environment,

00:13:45.530 --> 00:13:47.110
maybe from a
different perspective,

00:13:47.110 --> 00:13:50.380
and also use it to think
about capacity planning,

00:13:50.380 --> 00:13:54.160
longer-term trends, et cetera.

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:58.060
So a few years ago, our attempt
to build this particular system

00:13:58.060 --> 00:14:00.140
looked a little bit like this.

00:14:00.140 --> 00:14:02.770
So what we had was a set of
third party and open source

00:14:02.770 --> 00:14:05.830
collectors that would collect
all kinds of different data,

00:14:05.830 --> 00:14:07.900
ports and protocols
and things like that,

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:10.420
syslog, HTTP, et cetera.

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:13.900
Most of that raw data
fed our Kafka pipeline.

00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:16.570
And then we would
take that raw data

00:14:16.570 --> 00:14:19.780
and put it into Hadoop
for long-term storage

00:14:19.780 --> 00:14:22.930
and feed it to a set of third
party log analysis tools

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:25.520
for real-time query and search.

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:27.520
Unfortunately, there were
a number of challenges

00:14:27.520 --> 00:14:28.960
for this approach.

00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:33.190
The biggest challenge was
the increasing data volume

00:14:33.190 --> 00:14:35.290
that we were seeing
each and every day.

00:14:35.290 --> 00:14:36.940
When we originally
built this system,

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:40.420
we were anticipating a few
terabytes of data per day.

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:42.460
But we quickly
exceeded that and were

00:14:42.460 --> 00:14:46.870
moving into the tens and dozens
of terabytes of data per day.

00:14:46.870 --> 00:14:49.510
And all of this data
had a significant impact

00:14:49.510 --> 00:14:51.560
on our query performance.

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:53.920
Especially dense data,
like network flow traffic

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:56.620
or those sorts of things,
would take a really long time

00:14:56.620 --> 00:14:58.522
to complete, hours
in some cases.

00:14:58.522 --> 00:15:00.730
And of course, that was a
very frustrating experience

00:15:00.730 --> 00:15:01.313
for our users.

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:06.310
Retention periods
was also a problem,

00:15:06.310 --> 00:15:08.230
as the more data we
collected, we really

00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:11.540
couldn't keep up and offer our
users the level of retention

00:15:11.540 --> 00:15:12.610
they wanted.

00:15:12.610 --> 00:15:15.850
In some cases, we were
only able to keep logs

00:15:15.850 --> 00:15:18.880
for just a few days, which was
really frustrating for teams

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:21.880
that wanted to be able to do
comparative analysis, which

00:15:21.880 --> 00:15:24.130
is really important in
a retail environment.

00:15:24.130 --> 00:15:27.190
We're really interested in
looking at how today looks like

00:15:27.190 --> 00:15:30.340
compared to last week or
maybe the same day last month

00:15:30.340 --> 00:15:32.230
or even the same day last year.

00:15:32.230 --> 00:15:34.090
And the limits we
had in our system

00:15:34.090 --> 00:15:38.940
just made it impossible to
do those sorts of analysis.

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:40.590
And then, finally,
all this data meant

00:15:40.590 --> 00:15:43.230
we had all this
infrastructure to deal with.

00:15:43.230 --> 00:15:44.940
And all this
infrastructure meant

00:15:44.940 --> 00:15:46.920
that we had lots of
problems and lots

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:49.668
of time spent dealing
with the infrastructure.

00:15:49.668 --> 00:15:51.210
All of the time that
we were spending

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:53.490
dealing on infrastructure
was less time

00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:56.190
that we were able to spend
taking care of our users,

00:15:56.190 --> 00:15:58.410
improving their experience.

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:01.170
I know you've probably
heard the term data lake.

00:16:01.170 --> 00:16:03.840
But when we built this system,
and all these challenges

00:16:03.840 --> 00:16:06.007
that we were experiencing,
it felt a little bit more

00:16:06.007 --> 00:16:07.030
like a data dumpster.

00:16:07.030 --> 00:16:08.840
We were just dumping
all this data in,

00:16:08.840 --> 00:16:13.240
and we were really unable
to derive any value from it.

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:16.240
So we decided it was
time for a new approach.

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:18.960
And so what we did was
we considered the problem

00:16:18.960 --> 00:16:20.350
as four steps.

00:16:20.350 --> 00:16:22.350
So the first step is
to collect the data.

00:16:22.350 --> 00:16:24.090
All that raw data
that's floating around

00:16:24.090 --> 00:16:26.340
in our environment, we
need a way to collect it.

00:16:26.340 --> 00:16:29.170
And we had a pretty good
sense of how to do that.

00:16:29.170 --> 00:16:31.410
The second step is to
transform that data.

00:16:31.410 --> 00:16:33.570
We wanted to take
the raw data, turn it

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:35.490
into some sort of usable format.

00:16:35.490 --> 00:16:36.670
It might be semi-structured.

00:16:36.670 --> 00:16:38.110
It might be structured.

00:16:38.110 --> 00:16:41.460
But somehow we needed
to make it usable.

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:44.070
The third step was we needed
to store the data somewhere.

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:47.280
Stick it somewhere that
we could reason with it,

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:49.560
interact with it, et cetera.

00:16:49.560 --> 00:16:50.970
The last step is analyze.

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:53.870
And this is the one that's
really, really important.

00:16:53.870 --> 00:16:56.605
Analyze is a word
that gets used a lot.

00:16:56.605 --> 00:16:58.230
But really, it's just
the idea that you

00:16:58.230 --> 00:17:01.980
want to be able to search and
interact and explore your data.

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:04.589
You want to have a conversation
with it, if you will.

00:17:04.589 --> 00:17:06.000
And we really
wanted to make sure

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:09.270
that any system that we
decided on building, any system

00:17:09.270 --> 00:17:12.810
that we developed, allowed our
users to have that conversation

00:17:12.810 --> 00:17:14.733
with their data.

00:17:14.733 --> 00:17:16.650
So like any good software
engineering problem,

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:18.349
we cut the problem in half.

00:17:18.349 --> 00:17:20.700
And what we did was we
decided to keep our collection

00:17:20.700 --> 00:17:23.083
and transformation
functions in-house,

00:17:23.083 --> 00:17:24.750
which made sense,
because we had already

00:17:24.750 --> 00:17:28.200
spent a lot of engineering
time working on that problem.

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:30.750
We had a lot of collectors
and aggregation tiers

00:17:30.750 --> 00:17:32.820
in place to get the data.

00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:35.993
And there are so many remote
sites and systems at Home Depot

00:17:35.993 --> 00:17:38.160
that it didn't really make
sense for all the systems

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:40.328
to send straight to the cloud.

00:17:40.328 --> 00:17:42.120
The transformation
layer also was something

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:43.495
that we started
to keep in-house,

00:17:43.495 --> 00:17:46.170
because we needed to make sure
all the aggregation functions

00:17:46.170 --> 00:17:50.010
were there and turn it into
some sort of structured data.

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:51.930
But the store and
analyze functions,

00:17:51.930 --> 00:17:54.250
we felt like we could
move those to the cloud

00:17:54.250 --> 00:17:56.700
and that would be a win for us.

00:17:56.700 --> 00:18:00.560
And so we started looking
at ways to do that.

00:18:00.560 --> 00:18:02.230
And we felt that
when we did that,

00:18:02.230 --> 00:18:05.190
we would be able to optimize
cost and really improve

00:18:05.190 --> 00:18:07.200
our resource efficiency.

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:08.640
If we were able
to move the store

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:10.830
and analyze functions
to the cloud,

00:18:10.830 --> 00:18:13.800
we felt like our engineers could
spend a lot more time focused

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:15.450
on getting the
data in and making

00:18:15.450 --> 00:18:18.270
it valuable to our
users and less time

00:18:18.270 --> 00:18:20.580
on dealing with the
infrastructure itself.

00:18:20.580 --> 00:18:24.540
Plus, if we were able to use
a managed service for storing

00:18:24.540 --> 00:18:26.850
and analyzing data,
we felt like we

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:29.730
would be able to really
take advantage of the scale

00:18:29.730 --> 00:18:31.992
and elasticity of the cloud.

00:18:31.992 --> 00:18:33.450
So these are sort
of the guidelines

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:35.992
that we were considering as we
were looking at a new approach

00:18:35.992 --> 00:18:36.940
here.

00:18:36.940 --> 00:18:39.210
And so we started our
evaluation process

00:18:39.210 --> 00:18:41.530
with a number of
different considerations.

00:18:41.530 --> 00:18:44.790
So the first is, what are
the features of the platform

00:18:44.790 --> 00:18:46.680
that we're interested in?

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:48.540
And first and
foremost, we needed

00:18:48.540 --> 00:18:50.693
to make sure that it was
going to handle our scale.

00:18:50.693 --> 00:18:52.110
We wanted to make
sure that it was

00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:54.810
going to start with
the tens of terabytes

00:18:54.810 --> 00:18:57.990
that we were getting right
when we started, but was going

00:18:57.990 --> 00:19:00.540
to be able to grow with us
for all the volume and all

00:19:00.540 --> 00:19:02.773
the variety of data
that we collect.

00:19:02.773 --> 00:19:04.440
We have hundreds of
different data types

00:19:04.440 --> 00:19:05.880
that we're pulling
in the system.

00:19:05.880 --> 00:19:09.210
We needed to make sure that
it was capable of handling

00:19:09.210 --> 00:19:10.327
all of that data.

00:19:10.327 --> 00:19:11.910
And we needed to
make sure that it had

00:19:11.910 --> 00:19:13.810
multiple modes of ingestion.

00:19:13.810 --> 00:19:17.430
We often have cases where we
need to backfill data or bulk

00:19:17.430 --> 00:19:19.710
load data or put data
into the system in lots

00:19:19.710 --> 00:19:21.160
of different ways.

00:19:21.160 --> 00:19:24.020
We also have cases where we
need to stream it in real time

00:19:24.020 --> 00:19:26.335
and our users need immediate
access to that data.

00:19:26.335 --> 00:19:28.710
So it was really important
that whatever system we picked

00:19:28.710 --> 00:19:32.433
had different ways of getting
that data into the system.

00:19:32.433 --> 00:19:34.350
The second thing we
considered was how well it

00:19:34.350 --> 00:19:35.985
integrated with other systems.

00:19:35.985 --> 00:19:39.270
It was important for our
users to have a fairly shallow

00:19:39.270 --> 00:19:41.960
learning curve if we adopted
a whole new system that they

00:19:41.960 --> 00:19:43.730
were going to interact with.

00:19:43.730 --> 00:19:47.220
And so we wanted something that
was at least familiar with them

00:19:47.220 --> 00:19:50.000
and didn't require a
lot of upfront learning.

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:52.080
And secondly, we
wanted to make sure

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:54.750
that our users could
bring their own tools

00:19:54.750 --> 00:19:56.850
to the data for analytics.

00:19:56.850 --> 00:20:00.270
That might be anything
from a spreadsheet

00:20:00.270 --> 00:20:03.767
to BI tools to code.

00:20:03.767 --> 00:20:05.850
We wanted to make sure
that they had those options

00:20:05.850 --> 00:20:09.100
available to them.

00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:11.610
And then, of course, the
third thing was security.

00:20:11.610 --> 00:20:13.450
As we were moving to
a managed service,

00:20:13.450 --> 00:20:15.210
it was obviously
very important for us

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:18.150
to ensure that data to
and from that system

00:20:18.150 --> 00:20:20.820
was encrypted and
secure, and it was

00:20:20.820 --> 00:20:23.328
being stored in the same way.

00:20:23.328 --> 00:20:24.870
And of course, we
wanted to make sure

00:20:24.870 --> 00:20:28.860
that our data was able
to be segmented up.

00:20:28.860 --> 00:20:30.360
Because we had these
different teams

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:32.327
of users that need
access that data,

00:20:32.327 --> 00:20:34.910
we wanted to make sure that we
could authorize different users

00:20:34.910 --> 00:20:36.327
to different
segments of the data,

00:20:36.327 --> 00:20:39.322
and make sure we could
split it up easily.

00:20:39.322 --> 00:20:41.530
And then, of course, finally,
with a managed service,

00:20:41.530 --> 00:20:43.697
we wanted to make sure it
reduces costs and improves

00:20:43.697 --> 00:20:45.010
efficiencies.

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:47.140
And that's the idea behind
a fully managed service.

00:20:47.140 --> 00:20:49.953
We wanted to ensure that we
had a fully managed service

00:20:49.953 --> 00:20:51.370
and it wasn't
something that we're

00:20:51.370 --> 00:20:55.180
going to have to tinker with
or babysit or watch over.

00:20:55.180 --> 00:20:57.070
And of course, as
a managed service,

00:20:57.070 --> 00:21:00.490
we wanted to make sure that
it wasn't so narrow in focus

00:21:00.490 --> 00:21:02.890
that we could only use
it for a few use cases.

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:06.692
We wanted to make sure that it
covered as many as possible.

00:21:06.692 --> 00:21:08.900
It might surprise you to
learn that BigQuery was not,

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:10.970
in fact, the first option
that we considered.

00:21:10.970 --> 00:21:13.460
We actually spent a
significant amount

00:21:13.460 --> 00:21:16.070
of time evaluating
lots of options.

00:21:16.070 --> 00:21:19.280
And being in the operational
telemetry and log collection

00:21:19.280 --> 00:21:21.590
space, we started
with a lot of services

00:21:21.590 --> 00:21:25.730
that are in that space,
log collection services.

00:21:25.730 --> 00:21:28.670
As it turned out,
BigQuery was really

00:21:28.670 --> 00:21:31.470
the only one that met all
of these considerations.

00:21:31.470 --> 00:21:33.980
And so we felt pretty
confident in its ability

00:21:33.980 --> 00:21:35.750
to cover all these use cases.

00:21:35.750 --> 00:21:37.940
And we felt like we
could move forward

00:21:37.940 --> 00:21:40.010
with BigQuery as
a managed service,

00:21:40.010 --> 00:21:43.280
as its ability to put
vast amounts of data

00:21:43.280 --> 00:21:44.670
without falling over.

00:21:44.670 --> 00:21:46.962
And we also felt confident
that we'd be able to partner

00:21:46.962 --> 00:21:50.390
with Google to discuss
and cover any challenges

00:21:50.390 --> 00:21:53.858
that we might expect.

00:21:53.858 --> 00:21:55.400
So this is what we
ended up building.

00:21:55.400 --> 00:21:57.567
And this system is actually
in production right now.

00:21:57.567 --> 00:22:00.570
It's been in production
for about a year.

00:22:00.570 --> 00:22:04.190
What we ended up doing was
we kept our collection tier

00:22:04.190 --> 00:22:07.850
on-prem, same data
systems going into Kafka.

00:22:07.850 --> 00:22:10.100
We replaced the
third-party code that we

00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:12.800
had doing transformations
with some new code

00:22:12.800 --> 00:22:14.540
that we built in-house.

00:22:14.540 --> 00:22:17.310
And what it does is it
takes all of that raw data

00:22:17.310 --> 00:22:19.730
and it turns it into
structured data using

00:22:19.730 --> 00:22:22.220
Avro serialization, which
is something that BigQuery

00:22:22.220 --> 00:22:24.230
understands natively.

00:22:24.230 --> 00:22:25.880
All of those serialized
files are then

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:28.010
uploaded to Cloud
Storage, and then we

00:22:28.010 --> 00:22:31.250
use the BigQuery batch loading
API to load the data every 20

00:22:31.250 --> 00:22:32.450
seconds into BigQuery.

00:22:35.850 --> 00:22:38.490
One of the reasons that we
wanted to use structured data

00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:39.990
here is that it
gives us the ability

00:22:39.990 --> 00:22:42.360
to separate out our data sets.

00:22:42.360 --> 00:22:45.150
The challenge of the old
system was all the data

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:47.820
was in this data
dumpster and it was

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:51.160
very difficult to find the
data you're looking for.

00:22:51.160 --> 00:22:54.790
Once we were able to transform
the data into structured data

00:22:54.790 --> 00:22:56.727
sets, users could go
right to the table

00:22:56.727 --> 00:22:59.310
that they were interested in to
start querying and interacting

00:22:59.310 --> 00:23:01.060
with their data.

00:23:01.060 --> 00:23:03.570
So as I mentioned, this
particular system's

00:23:03.570 --> 00:23:07.050
been in place for about a
year and we are already seeing

00:23:07.050 --> 00:23:09.720
some pretty significant wins.

00:23:09.720 --> 00:23:12.000
The first, of course,
is data volume.

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:15.540
And we're easily able to put
tens of terabytes of data

00:23:15.540 --> 00:23:17.220
into the system
each and every day.

00:23:17.220 --> 00:23:18.595
And we're confident
that we'll be

00:23:18.595 --> 00:23:22.550
able to put more and more
data as it needs to go.

00:23:22.550 --> 00:23:24.970
The second is query performance.

00:23:24.970 --> 00:23:28.150
As we said earlier, our
enterprise data warehouse team

00:23:28.150 --> 00:23:30.040
was already using BigQuery.

00:23:30.040 --> 00:23:31.990
And so when we
selected BigQuery,

00:23:31.990 --> 00:23:35.200
we were able to take advantage
of some organizational

00:23:35.200 --> 00:23:37.310
economies of scale.

00:23:37.310 --> 00:23:40.060
One of the ways that we get
the scale from BigQuery is we

00:23:40.060 --> 00:23:42.670
had these things called query
slots, which is basically

00:23:42.670 --> 00:23:45.252
like a fixed Compute Engine.

00:23:45.252 --> 00:23:46.960
The more query slots
you have, the faster

00:23:46.960 --> 00:23:48.955
performance on BigQuery.

00:23:48.955 --> 00:23:50.710
And because the
enterprise data warehouse

00:23:50.710 --> 00:23:54.220
is running on BigQuery and
all of our operational data's

00:23:54.220 --> 00:23:57.040
on BigQuery, both teams
were able to take advantage

00:23:57.040 --> 00:23:59.800
of that pool of query slots,
so that whether you're

00:23:59.800 --> 00:24:02.200
querying for operational
data or if you're

00:24:02.200 --> 00:24:03.910
querying for
enterprise data, you

00:24:03.910 --> 00:24:07.540
get to take advantage of
the extra performance that's

00:24:07.540 --> 00:24:09.610
afforded to each
of the data sets.

00:24:09.610 --> 00:24:12.640
So that was a huge win for us.

00:24:12.640 --> 00:24:15.820
After we had moved a lot of
our data sets over to BigQuery,

00:24:15.820 --> 00:24:20.110
one of my engineers was working
on a problem with some network

00:24:20.110 --> 00:24:21.010
data.

00:24:21.010 --> 00:24:24.580
And he wanted some help
translating his query

00:24:24.580 --> 00:24:27.602
from the old system
over to the new system.

00:24:27.602 --> 00:24:29.560
And this was a query that
was looking at, like,

00:24:29.560 --> 00:24:31.150
30 days of network traffic.

00:24:31.150 --> 00:24:33.400
So it was a fairly
large data set.

00:24:33.400 --> 00:24:35.740
And it was looking at
traffic to and from

00:24:35.740 --> 00:24:38.740
a particular organization that
had maybe 60 or 70 different

00:24:38.740 --> 00:24:39.790
[INAUDIBLE].

00:24:39.790 --> 00:24:41.860
So kind of a complicated query.

00:24:41.860 --> 00:24:44.450
I'm not that great at SQL, but
I said I'll take a look at it

00:24:44.450 --> 00:24:46.130
and see if I can make it work.

00:24:46.130 --> 00:24:48.790
So I sat down and tinkered
with it for a little bit.

00:24:48.790 --> 00:24:51.212
And I got something running,
at least in the system,

00:24:51.212 --> 00:24:53.170
and it seemed to sort of
meet the needs of what

00:24:53.170 --> 00:24:54.670
he was looking for.

00:24:54.670 --> 00:24:56.832
But it was running in
about maybe five minutes.

00:24:56.832 --> 00:24:58.540
I didn't know what
his expectations were.

00:24:58.540 --> 00:24:59.200
So I went back to him.

00:24:59.200 --> 00:25:01.060
I said, hey, I've
worked on your query.

00:25:01.060 --> 00:25:02.740
I think it's working.

00:25:02.740 --> 00:25:04.552
It's taking about
five minutes to run.

00:25:04.552 --> 00:25:06.010
So if you want it
to run faster, we

00:25:06.010 --> 00:25:08.620
should probably get someone else
involved who's better at SQL

00:25:08.620 --> 00:25:09.367
than me.

00:25:09.367 --> 00:25:10.450
And he said, five minutes?

00:25:10.450 --> 00:25:11.172
Are you sure?

00:25:11.172 --> 00:25:12.130
I said, yeah, I'm sure.

00:25:12.130 --> 00:25:12.880
You can come look at it.

00:25:12.880 --> 00:25:13.380
I said, why?

00:25:13.380 --> 00:25:15.850
How long was it taking
in the last system?

00:25:15.850 --> 00:25:16.630
His response?

00:25:16.630 --> 00:25:17.290
Five hours.

00:25:22.310 --> 00:25:24.640
So with BigQuery,
the retention period

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:26.590
is another big win for us.

00:25:26.590 --> 00:25:29.200
Retention period in BigQuery
is really just a setting.

00:25:29.200 --> 00:25:31.750
It's not months of
infrastructure planning

00:25:31.750 --> 00:25:33.430
or hardware build,
at least not for us.

00:25:33.430 --> 00:25:35.080
Maybe on the Google side it is.

00:25:35.080 --> 00:25:38.350
But from our perspective, it's
just a setting on the table.

00:25:38.350 --> 00:25:42.010
And so, by default, we're able
to offer 90 days, by default,

00:25:42.010 --> 00:25:44.380
for all of our
customer data sets.

00:25:44.380 --> 00:25:46.430
And for some data sets,
like security data sets,

00:25:46.430 --> 00:25:48.880
we keep for years.

00:25:48.880 --> 00:25:51.610
And so all of the data
sets that we had before,

00:25:51.610 --> 00:25:53.950
which were only
available for a few days,

00:25:53.950 --> 00:25:57.100
now our teams are able to
do that comparative analysis

00:25:57.100 --> 00:25:58.610
that they were asking for.

00:25:58.610 --> 00:26:01.680
So that's been a
huge win for us.

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:04.170
And infrastructure maintenance.

00:26:04.170 --> 00:26:05.670
By moving to
BigQuery, we have been

00:26:05.670 --> 00:26:09.780
able to eliminate 10 petabytes
of Hadoop infrastructure, which

00:26:09.780 --> 00:26:11.530
was a really big win for us.

00:26:14.190 --> 00:26:16.830
So a few observations
now that this system

00:26:16.830 --> 00:26:20.107
has been running in
production for about a year.

00:26:20.107 --> 00:26:21.690
So I mentioned some
of the custom code

00:26:21.690 --> 00:26:23.130
that we were writing.

00:26:23.130 --> 00:26:25.350
We tried really
hard to stick with

00:26:25.350 --> 00:26:29.450
third-party and open-source
code for as long as possible.

00:26:29.450 --> 00:26:32.100
Writing these custom codes takes
longer than you think it will.

00:26:32.100 --> 00:26:33.180
It always kind of runs.

00:26:33.180 --> 00:26:35.323
You run into a lot of
unexpected problems

00:26:35.323 --> 00:26:36.990
more frequently than
you think you will.

00:26:36.990 --> 00:26:39.780
And so we tried really hard
to stick with that custom code

00:26:39.780 --> 00:26:42.690
for as long as possible and
only write our own custom

00:26:42.690 --> 00:26:45.960
code when we really had to.

00:26:45.960 --> 00:26:50.910
The second thing is, use
managed services where possible.

00:26:50.910 --> 00:26:53.400
With BigQuery, there's a
whole class of problems

00:26:53.400 --> 00:26:55.320
that I don't have
to think about.

00:26:55.320 --> 00:26:58.050
How data's stored,
how it's replicated,

00:26:58.050 --> 00:26:59.760
all the search and
query performance

00:26:59.760 --> 00:27:01.710
and the engineering
behind that, that's

00:27:01.710 --> 00:27:04.110
all something that the Google
team gets to worry about.

00:27:04.110 --> 00:27:07.510
I get to just take advantage
of that architecture.

00:27:07.510 --> 00:27:10.530
And so it's now easy for us to
load tens of terabytes a day.

00:27:10.530 --> 00:27:13.320
The data retention is simply
a setting for a table.

00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:17.283
And our query performance
is from hours and days

00:27:17.283 --> 00:27:18.450
down to seconds and minutes.

00:27:20.930 --> 00:27:22.430
All of these things
are really good.

00:27:22.430 --> 00:27:25.010
But even in our current
system, there are some things

00:27:25.010 --> 00:27:26.870
that we want to improve.

00:27:26.870 --> 00:27:29.480
The first thing is that
we experience some delays

00:27:29.480 --> 00:27:31.190
in data availability.

00:27:31.190 --> 00:27:34.040
I mentioned this is
for operational data.

00:27:34.040 --> 00:27:36.080
Operational data is
like a fire hose.

00:27:36.080 --> 00:27:37.620
It never, ever stops.

00:27:37.620 --> 00:27:40.760
And so if there's any
problem in our pipeline, what

00:27:40.760 --> 00:27:43.010
tends to happen
is the recent data

00:27:43.010 --> 00:27:46.460
gets stuck behind all of the
data that was in before it.

00:27:46.460 --> 00:27:48.500
And so we get this
backlog of data,

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:50.610
where users are sort of
twiddling their thumbs,

00:27:50.610 --> 00:27:52.170
waiting on their data.

00:27:52.170 --> 00:27:55.190
It's not a very good
experience for them.

00:27:55.190 --> 00:27:58.640
Also, on one of
Sap's earlier slides,

00:27:58.640 --> 00:28:01.670
there's a comment that says
free bulk loading, which

00:28:01.670 --> 00:28:03.500
is true up to a point.

00:28:03.500 --> 00:28:05.240
When you're loading
this much data,

00:28:05.240 --> 00:28:08.540
you actually have to get these
things called load slots, which

00:28:08.540 --> 00:28:10.100
are these sort of
compute units that

00:28:10.100 --> 00:28:11.840
allow you to load
lots and lots of data

00:28:11.840 --> 00:28:14.060
into BigQuery in batch mode.

00:28:14.060 --> 00:28:15.440
As it turns out,
these load slots

00:28:15.440 --> 00:28:17.390
are a little bit
difficult to predict.

00:28:17.390 --> 00:28:19.200
We have this huge
fire hose of data.

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:23.180
So any sort of burst in data
can be difficult to predict

00:28:23.180 --> 00:28:26.690
and it can be tough to
make sure that we're not

00:28:26.690 --> 00:28:28.610
going to have an
operational impact when we

00:28:28.610 --> 00:28:30.830
have that giant burst of data.

00:28:30.830 --> 00:28:33.110
Also, and somewhat
unfortunately, load slots

00:28:33.110 --> 00:28:35.690
are incompatible
with query slots.

00:28:35.690 --> 00:28:37.640
So we were put in the
sort of awkward position

00:28:37.640 --> 00:28:40.610
of trying to spend resources
on either load slots or query

00:28:40.610 --> 00:28:43.700
slots and improving load times
or improving query times.

00:28:43.700 --> 00:28:45.200
So that was something
that we wanted

00:28:45.200 --> 00:28:50.342
to address on the next
evolution of this architecture.

00:28:50.342 --> 00:28:51.800
The other thing
that we experienced

00:28:51.800 --> 00:28:55.910
was a lot of quotas and
resource limits and things

00:28:55.910 --> 00:28:57.860
that we ran into.

00:28:57.860 --> 00:28:59.840
As we started
building the system,

00:28:59.840 --> 00:29:01.970
we would run into one
quota, work with the team

00:29:01.970 --> 00:29:04.730
to get it adjusted, and then run
head first into the next one.

00:29:04.730 --> 00:29:06.230
So there's a lot
of things that need

00:29:06.230 --> 00:29:08.570
to be tuned and adjusted
to deal with the scale

00:29:08.570 --> 00:29:11.510
and volume of data that
we were interested in.

00:29:11.510 --> 00:29:13.220
We also weren't
nearly creative enough

00:29:13.220 --> 00:29:15.020
thinking about the
types of failures

00:29:15.020 --> 00:29:18.110
that we might run into
building the system.

00:29:18.110 --> 00:29:20.060
One good example
is that we started

00:29:20.060 --> 00:29:23.040
with using regional buckets
on cloud storage for all

00:29:23.040 --> 00:29:24.530
of those Avro files.

00:29:24.530 --> 00:29:26.840
One regional outage
taught us to quickly move

00:29:26.840 --> 00:29:29.340
to multi-regional storage.

00:29:29.340 --> 00:29:32.090
The second thing is to really
think about fault domains.

00:29:32.090 --> 00:29:34.370
One mistake we made
building the system was we

00:29:34.370 --> 00:29:37.460
built essentially
one giant pipeline.

00:29:37.460 --> 00:29:40.370
That was a mistake, because
any impact in the system

00:29:40.370 --> 00:29:42.820
impacted all of our customers.

00:29:42.820 --> 00:29:44.570
So one of the things
that we're working on

00:29:44.570 --> 00:29:46.740
is splitting that up into
smaller fault domains

00:29:46.740 --> 00:29:48.740
to limit the number of
people that are mad at us

00:29:48.740 --> 00:29:49.490
at any given time.

00:29:53.110 --> 00:29:56.940
So the design for the new
system is actually already done.

00:29:56.940 --> 00:29:58.380
And it's in testing right now.

00:29:58.380 --> 00:30:01.810
And the plan is to move to
it in the next few months.

00:30:01.810 --> 00:30:04.980
So this is what our next phase
of the architecture looks like.

00:30:04.980 --> 00:30:08.200
We're essentially moving
more to a streaming model.

00:30:08.200 --> 00:30:10.830
One of the things that
we did was that we

00:30:10.830 --> 00:30:12.360
rewrote our collection tier.

00:30:12.360 --> 00:30:15.780
We had all this third-party
and open-source tools

00:30:15.780 --> 00:30:18.242
that we were using, and it
was becoming more complicated

00:30:18.242 --> 00:30:19.450
and more difficult to manage.

00:30:19.450 --> 00:30:21.550
So we wrote our own
collection tier,

00:30:21.550 --> 00:30:24.030
which gave us the
ability to classify data

00:30:24.030 --> 00:30:25.880
as it was being streamed in.

00:30:25.880 --> 00:30:28.620
And that way we were able
to separate this data out

00:30:28.620 --> 00:30:30.960
as it feeds into
Kafka, and then we

00:30:30.960 --> 00:30:33.570
could upload that data
already classified up

00:30:33.570 --> 00:30:35.250
into Cloud Storage.

00:30:35.250 --> 00:30:37.440
That gave us a single
ingestion tier to worry about.

00:30:37.440 --> 00:30:39.690
And we were able to
get consistent stats

00:30:39.690 --> 00:30:42.750
and performance across
the entire tier.

00:30:42.750 --> 00:30:46.950
From there, the process
is very similar.

00:30:46.950 --> 00:30:49.340
All of the data that
goes into Cloud Storage,

00:30:49.340 --> 00:30:52.260
it is already in
those Avro files

00:30:52.260 --> 00:30:53.730
that I talked about earlier.

00:30:53.730 --> 00:30:56.430
But once the file is created,
we generate a notification

00:30:56.430 --> 00:30:58.590
that goes into Pub/Sub.

00:30:58.590 --> 00:31:02.870
Our transformation tier is now
running on Kubernetes Engine.

00:31:02.870 --> 00:31:04.620
And so it's just using
a pull subscription

00:31:04.620 --> 00:31:07.140
to listen for those
notifications.

00:31:07.140 --> 00:31:08.910
Once it gets one,
it reads the file

00:31:08.910 --> 00:31:13.380
from Cloud Storage that
streams the data into BigQuery.

00:31:13.380 --> 00:31:15.450
Some of the advantages
of this new system.

00:31:18.340 --> 00:31:20.140
We're getting much
faster data availability

00:31:20.140 --> 00:31:24.160
with BigQuery streaming than
we were with the batch loads.

00:31:24.160 --> 00:31:26.795
In some cases, data is now
available in seconds as opposed

00:31:26.795 --> 00:31:27.295
to minutes.

00:31:29.950 --> 00:31:33.190
Because we've moved a lot of our
transform workload over to GKE,

00:31:33.190 --> 00:31:35.200
we're now able to take
advantage of things

00:31:35.200 --> 00:31:38.410
like Kubernetes pod
autoscaling to grow and shrink

00:31:38.410 --> 00:31:40.670
our compute resources
over the course of a day.

00:31:43.400 --> 00:31:48.650
And this particular model
addresses the problem

00:31:48.650 --> 00:31:51.560
of the last model, which
was the data getting

00:31:51.560 --> 00:31:55.080
stuck behind that older data.

00:31:55.080 --> 00:31:56.030
So how does that work?

00:31:56.030 --> 00:31:58.380
Let me dive into that
for just a second.

00:31:58.380 --> 00:32:03.080
So the way this works is we
built essentially two paths

00:32:03.080 --> 00:32:05.090
for these notifications.

00:32:05.090 --> 00:32:08.810
And the notifications only
use one path at a time.

00:32:08.810 --> 00:32:14.030
So as the data is streamed up
and written into Cloud Storage,

00:32:14.030 --> 00:32:17.540
we generate those notifications
on either the A path or the B

00:32:17.540 --> 00:32:18.440
path.

00:32:18.440 --> 00:32:20.780
If one path starts
getting behind or there's

00:32:20.780 --> 00:32:23.000
a backlog or any
sort of problem,

00:32:23.000 --> 00:32:25.760
just like a railroad switch, we
just switch the notifications

00:32:25.760 --> 00:32:29.600
to the secondary
path, and from there,

00:32:29.600 --> 00:32:32.900
all the recent data can
be streamed into BigQuery,

00:32:32.900 --> 00:32:35.960
and the backlog data
can just flow in

00:32:35.960 --> 00:32:37.850
as it was flowing in before.

00:32:37.850 --> 00:32:39.800
But this gives us
the opportunity

00:32:39.800 --> 00:32:43.400
to keep recent data recent
and available for our users.

00:32:47.630 --> 00:32:50.470
So a few lessons learned
from our experience

00:32:50.470 --> 00:32:52.430
building this system.

00:32:52.430 --> 00:32:56.300
The first thing
is, keep it simple.

00:32:56.300 --> 00:32:58.450
There's a lot of complexity
in the system already.

00:32:58.450 --> 00:33:00.280
There's a lot of
options out there,

00:33:00.280 --> 00:33:03.850
a lot of tools and things to
play with and tinker with.

00:33:03.850 --> 00:33:06.370
But we only started
with two cloud services

00:33:06.370 --> 00:33:08.620
when we built this system,
Cloud Storage and BigQuery,

00:33:08.620 --> 00:33:10.008
that was it.

00:33:10.008 --> 00:33:12.550
And we didn't move forward with
any additional cloud services

00:33:12.550 --> 00:33:15.460
until we felt like we
had really understood

00:33:15.460 --> 00:33:18.880
those services and the impact
that those services was going

00:33:18.880 --> 00:33:22.090
to have on the overall system.

00:33:22.090 --> 00:33:25.210
All of the additional complexity
that you add to the system

00:33:25.210 --> 00:33:27.470
means that your failures are
going to be more complex.

00:33:27.470 --> 00:33:29.450
It's more your
team has to learn.

00:33:29.450 --> 00:33:33.190
So it's really important
to keep it simple.

00:33:33.190 --> 00:33:35.200
Using those fault domains
to limit the impact

00:33:35.200 --> 00:33:37.000
is another good lesson for us.

00:33:37.000 --> 00:33:40.860
Simplicity in how many
things can go wrong

00:33:40.860 --> 00:33:43.540
limit the overall
impact to the system

00:33:43.540 --> 00:33:45.260
when something does
inevitably go wrong.

00:33:50.170 --> 00:33:53.230
The second thing is, make
sure you iterate your design.

00:33:53.230 --> 00:33:55.300
You've seen a couple
of different evolutions

00:33:55.300 --> 00:33:58.490
of our architecture and what
we're about to implement.

00:33:58.490 --> 00:34:02.110
And I'm sure there'll be
changes down the road as well.

00:34:02.110 --> 00:34:04.575
You want to plan for
failures in your system.

00:34:04.575 --> 00:34:06.970
It's not going to be
perfect from day one.

00:34:06.970 --> 00:34:09.257
We certainly learned that.

00:34:09.257 --> 00:34:11.590
We want to use those failures
to help iterate our design

00:34:11.590 --> 00:34:14.260
and make those
incremental improvements.

00:34:14.260 --> 00:34:17.260
A lot of the quotas and limits
and stuff that we ran into,

00:34:17.260 --> 00:34:18.980
a lot of those can be adjusted.

00:34:18.980 --> 00:34:22.090
So it's important to stay
aligned with your customer

00:34:22.090 --> 00:34:24.969
engineering team to make sure
that you kind of stay ahead

00:34:24.969 --> 00:34:27.250
of those particular limits.

00:34:27.250 --> 00:34:30.139
And then consider your
workload placement over time.

00:34:30.139 --> 00:34:33.219
It doesn't have to be the
case that all of your workload

00:34:33.219 --> 00:34:35.560
moves to the cloud from day one.

00:34:35.560 --> 00:34:36.880
We certainly didn't.

00:34:36.880 --> 00:34:38.679
We started with a hybrid model.

00:34:38.679 --> 00:34:40.300
We're continuing
with a hybrid model.

00:34:40.300 --> 00:34:42.850
We have a lot of workload
that runs on-prem.

00:34:42.850 --> 00:34:45.310
We're moving different
pieces to the cloud

00:34:45.310 --> 00:34:47.440
as it fits our
particular use case.

00:34:47.440 --> 00:34:49.780
And you should feel free
to do the same thing.

00:34:49.780 --> 00:34:51.250
That is a good
model to work with.

00:34:53.800 --> 00:34:55.750
And lastly, focus on value.

00:34:55.750 --> 00:34:57.700
And what I mean
by this is really

00:34:57.700 --> 00:35:00.550
think more about
outcomes and what

00:35:00.550 --> 00:35:02.350
you're trying to get
to for your customers

00:35:02.350 --> 00:35:05.020
than the engineering effort.

00:35:05.020 --> 00:35:08.450
A lot of times we get excited by
the engineering, and that's OK.

00:35:08.450 --> 00:35:11.160
There's a lot of fun
toys to play with here.

00:35:11.160 --> 00:35:13.660
But it's important to really
think about the outcome of what

00:35:13.660 --> 00:35:16.225
you're trying to get to.

00:35:16.225 --> 00:35:18.850
When you're using something like
BigQuery as a managed service,

00:35:18.850 --> 00:35:21.550
it's important to realize
that there's a lot of things

00:35:21.550 --> 00:35:23.065
you don't have to worry about.

00:35:23.065 --> 00:35:25.480
You can immediately start
having that conversation

00:35:25.480 --> 00:35:28.318
with your data, and
your users will benefit.

00:35:28.318 --> 00:35:29.860
And then, of course,
think about ways

00:35:29.860 --> 00:35:32.440
to cut the problem in half,
to leverage your partners,

00:35:32.440 --> 00:35:37.300
and to make sure that you're
able to continue to use them

00:35:37.300 --> 00:35:40.270
as sort of an extension
of your engineering team

00:35:40.270 --> 00:35:42.950
as you focus on those outcomes.

00:35:42.950 --> 00:35:44.375
So that's really it.

00:35:44.375 --> 00:35:46.750
My team has certainly learned
a lot building this system.

00:35:46.750 --> 00:35:48.400
And hopefully, some
of these experiences

00:35:48.400 --> 00:35:49.775
will be helpful
for you if you're

00:35:49.775 --> 00:35:51.520
considering something similar.

00:35:51.520 --> 00:35:55.770
[MUSIC PLAYING]

